Design proposal — "axolotl, programmer edition":

```rust
// arithmetic.axo — programmer-flavored

type Nat = fn(X: Type) -> fn(fn(X) -> X) -> fn(X) -> X;

fn zero(X: Type, s: fn(X) -> X, z: X) -> X {
    z
}

fn succ(n: Nat) -> Nat {
    fn(X: Type, s: fn(X) -> X, z: X) -> X {
        s(n(X, s, z))
    }
}

let two: Nat = succ(succ(zero));
let four: Nat = succ(succ(two));

fn plus(m: Nat, n: Nat) -> Nat {
    fn(X: Type, s: fn(X) -> X, z: X) -> X {
        m(X, s, n(X, s, z))
    }
}

fn Eq(A: Type, x: A, y: A) -> Type {
    fn(P: fn(A) -> Type) -> fn(P(x)) -> P(y)
}

fn refl(A: Type, x: A) -> Eq(A, x, x) {
    fn(P: fn(A) -> Type, px: P(x)) -> P(x) {
        px
    }
}

theorem two_plus_two: Eq(Nat, plus(two, two), four) {
    refl(Nat, four)
}
```

This is quite readable! Key elements:
- `fn name(args) -> RetType { body }` for definitions — Rust-like
- `fn(args) -> Ret { body }` as lambda (anonymous fn, like Zig or JS `function`)
- `fn(A) -> B` as the function type (Pi) — like Rust's `Fn(A) -> B` or Zig's fn types
