Extend the context, then judge the body. That ritual *is* the scoping rule. Scattered twice as manual pushes, it's an unlabeled invariant; worse, `ctx` and `env` must always grow in **lockstep** (same length — position i must mean the same variable in both), and manual pushes can drift out of sync — a classic kernel bug.

## The helper

```rust
/// Enter a binder: ctx gains its type, env gains a fresh probe.
/// Returns the probe too, for callers that fire codomain closures.
fn under_binder(ctx: &Ctx, env: &Env, ty: ValueRef) -> (Ctx, Env, ValueRef) {
    let probe = fresh(env.len());               // the next unused level
    (ctx.extended(ty), env.extended(probe.clone()), probe)
}
```

Three parts, one per line of the doc comment:

1. **`fresh(env.len())`** — the probe for the new variable. `env.len()` is the next unused level (binders so far = level so far — the invariant from the NbE lessons)
2. **`ctx.extended(ty)` / `env.extended(...)`** — your new fluent trait doing the pushes, immutably
3. **Returns a triple** — because one caller needs the probe itself: `check`'s Lambda arm must fire the codomain closure *at the same probe* that goes into `env`. (Two handles, one neutral — the `.clone()` is the Rc share, so both are literally the same variable.)

## The arms after

```rust
